    "problem_statement": "# format_map method not working correctly in Markup class\n\nHi,\n\nI've found an issue with the `format_map` method in the `Markup` class. When trying to use this method to format strings with a dictionary, it doesn't work as expected.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nHere's a simple example that demonstrates the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom markupsafe import Markup\n\n# This should work but fails\nresult = Markup('<em>{value}</em>').format_map({'value': '<value>'})\nprint(result)\n```\n\nWhen running this code, instead of getting the expected output with the value properly escaped, I get a TypeError. The method seems to be passing arguments in the wrong order to the formatter.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe `format_map` method should work similar to the standard string's `format_map` method, properly escaping HTML in the values from the dictionary.\n\nExpected output should be:\n```\n<em>&lt;value&gt;</em>\n```\n\nThis issue appears to be in the implementation of the `format_map` method in the `Markup` class, where the arguments to `formatter.vformat()` might be in the wrong order."
